=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r2
diagnosis_text:
There is a real network fault, but the reported symptom is a false premise. PC0 (20.1.1.10) can reach PC12 (120.1.1.10) without any packet loss — five consecutive pings returned 5/5 and a traceroute traces a clean four-hop path (PC0 → r1 → r11 → r12 → PC12). No backbone link on the actual PC0→PC12 forwarding path is down.

The genuine fault lies on the r1–r2 backbone link (collision domain A). Both r1.eth0 and r2.eth0 are configured under OSPF Area 0.0.0.0 on the same shared segment, but they have been assigned IP addresses in entirely different /8 subnets: r1.eth0 carries `12.1.1.1/8` while r2.eth0 carries `1.1.1.2/8`. Because these addresses belong to different subnet ranges, OSPF Hello packets sent by each router are not accepted by the other as a valid neighbor — both interfaces present as DR with zero neighbors. As a result, r1 and r2 have never formed an OSPF adjacency on segment A; each reaches the other's directly-connected networks only via a two-hop detour through r3 (cost 30 rather than the expected cost 10). This is confirmed directly: r1's neighbor table lists only r3 (39.1.1.1 via eth2) and r11 (112.1.1.1 via eth1); r2 is not present.

The root cause is an IP address misconfiguration on r2.eth0: it was set to `1.1.1.2/8` during the maintenance window instead of an address within r1's `12.0.0.0/8` subnet (e.g., `12.1.1.2/8`). The corrective fix is to reconfigure r2.eth0 with `12.1.1.2/8`, which will allow OSPF Hellos to be accepted, restore the r1–r2 adjacency, and reduce the backbone routing cost for those prefixes. PC0→PC12 connectivity is unaffected because that path traverses r11 exclusively.
=== END ===